  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes artificial gauge fields in photonics and the broad areas where they are used. At the center is a circular diagram organized by three themes—scalar, vector, and matrix fields—together with complex and quantum aspects. The ring is color-coded to distinguish classical from quantum regimes, suggesting that artificial gauge fields connect both.\n\nAround this hub are five example directions. One shows transformation optics, where a curved coordinate grid and a bent red trajectory indicate that engineered media can redirect light as if space itself were warped. Another depicts topological photonics with two bands connected at a narrow point, representing protected band-structure features. A non-Abelian optics sketch uses intertwined strands, visually conveying matrix-valued gauge behavior and path-dependent evolution. The non-Hermitian optics example highlights an exceptional point (EP) and the non-Hermitian skin effect (NHSE), with modes appearing to accumulate toward one side. Finally, the quantum photonics panel shows a lattice with arrows near points labeled K and K′, implying valley- or spin-like gauge effects in a quantum setting.\n\nOverall, the image presents artificial gauge fields as a unifying framework for controlling light across several modern photonics subfields.",
